  {
    "id": "introduction",
    "title": "Introduction to Tajweed",
    "description": "Understanding the definition, purpose, and legal ruling of learning Tajweed.",
    "lessons": [
      {
        "id": "def_tajwid",
        "title": "Definition of Tajweed",
        "definition": "Linguistically, Tajweed means 'improvement' or 'to make something better'.\n\nTerminologically, it means: 'Giving every letter its rights and dues from its articulation point.'\n\n'Rights of the letter' (Haqq al-Huruf) refers to its permanent characteristics that never change, such as Al-Jahr, Isti'la', and Istifal.\n\n'Dues of the letter' (Mustahakk al-Huruf) refers to temporary characteristics that appear in certain conditions, such as Tafkhim (heavy), Tarqiq (light), and Ikhfa'.",
         "letters": [],
         "examples": []
      },
      {
        "id": "hukum_tajwid",
        "title": "Ruling on Learning Tajweed",
        "definition": "The theoretical knowledge of Tajweed rules is Fardhu Kifayah (Communal Obligation) – meaning if some members of the community learn it, the obligation is lifted from the rest.\n\nHowever, reading the Quran with proper Tajweed application is Fardhu 'Ain (Individual Obligation) for every Muslim.\n\nIt is possible for a reciter to read correctly without knowing the technical terms (like Idzhar or Mad). But for those who cannot read correctly, it is mandatory to strive to improve their recitation to meet the standard set by the Prophet Muhammad Sholallohu'alaihi wasallam.",
         "letters": [],
         "examples": []
      }
    ]
  },
